\subsection{Cross-Strategy Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossstrategy}

Cross-strategy transfer reveals a structural barrier: d1--d3 balanced accuracy drops to 45.9\% (four-class: 59.1\%), 7\,pp below cross-model transfer (Table~\ref{tab:cross_strategy}).
A two-stage pipeline (strategy classifier $\to$ conditioned depth classifier) recovers much of this gap: 70.5\% four-class accuracy ($+$11.5\,pp over LOO); oracle routing yields 78.3\%, so the 7.8\,pp residual is attributable to strategy misidentification (Appendix~\ref{app:extended_discussion}).
Our depth estimation claims hold most strongly within-strategy or when the decoding configuration is known.

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Preliminary Cross-Scale Observations (14B)}
\label{sec:gen:crossscale}

At 14B (Qwen2.5-14B), self-reference yields 50.8\% ($2{\times}$ chance, $\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}{=}0$); cross-reference control (Pythia-1.4B) drops accuracy to 44.1\% ($-$6.8\,pp) but raises $\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}$ to 2 for nucleus and temperature, revealing that ${\sim}$7\,pp of the accuracy drop is attributable to reference-model mismatch rather than scale (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_scale_full}).
Cross-domain (arXiv) accuracy reaches 74.1\% at 1--1.5B; cross-domain FPR is discussed in Limitations.

\section*{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\paragraph{Full-replacement vs.\ accumulation.}
The primary benchmark uses full-replacement chains; real-world contamination involves accumulation at unknown mixing ratios \cite{gerstgrasser2024_accumulate}.
At 25--50\% synthetic mixing, accuracy drops 8--15\,pp and $\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}{=}3$ is preserved in 5/6 tested cells (GPT-2~XL nucleus at 50\% drops to $\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}{=}2$; d2v3 AUC\,=\,0.695, marginally below threshold; Table~\ref{tab:accum_auc}); accumulation experiments use matched strategies per model (nucleus for GPT-2~XL/Pythia, temperature for OLMo) and do not test cross-strategy accumulation; extension to larger models and higher mixing ratios remains future work.

\paragraph{Scale, generalization, and reference model.}
At 14B, cross-reference accuracy drops to 44.1\% ($\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}{=}2$), with reference-model mismatch accounting for ${\sim}$7\,pp; cross-strategy transfer (d1--d3: 45.9\%) can invert d1/d2 predictions (d1v2 AUC as low as 0.13 for temperature text, 0.25 for OLMo, classified by a nucleus-trained model), requiring strategy-aware deployment.
The pipeline requires a fixed reference model; cross-architecture references degrade accuracy by 4--11\,pp (Appendix~\ref{app:ref_ablation}).
Additional caveats (single LoRA seed with ${\sim}$1--3\,pp uncertainty, web-crawl false positive rates, passage length sensitivity) are detailed in the Appendix.

\section*{Ethics Statement}

This work uses publicly available pretrained language models (Pythia-1.4B, OLMo-1B, GPT-2~XL) and Wikipedia text as seed data.
No human subjects were involved, no private or sensitive data were collected, and no new models were released.
Our method is intended to support data provenance and training-data auditing; we acknowledge that depth estimation tools could in principle be misused to circumvent content moderation, though such risks are shared with all detection-oriented research.
Code and data will be released upon acceptance.
AI assistants were used in an auxiliary role for coding support and language polishing.
All scientific decisions, experimental design, analysis, and core writing were conducted by the authors.

